WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.070
Film Courage: What is ‘meaningful conflict’
and why do so many screenwriters get this

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.070
wrong?

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:10.250
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: So meaningful
conflict…conflict to me is synonymous with

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.250
problems.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.630
So I remember early in my screenwriting endeavors
I remember people saying to me “Your script

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.340
doesn’t have enough conflict.”

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.940
And I was like “I don’t understand what
that means?”

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:24.320
I thought like that just meant people fighting
and arguing or beating up on each other.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.430
But then I came to understand that conflict
and problem mean the same thing so in any

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:33.261
story the audience is most interested and
engaged when there is some active problem

00:00:33.261 --> 00:00:38.390
that someone is actively trying to resolve,
That’s pretty much like Scene Writing 101

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.390
/ Story 101.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.270
You always want to be focused on that.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:46.350
And when there isn’t a problem to be solved
or whenever no one is trying to solve it (even

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.790
if it exists) audiences emotional engagement
tends to sag a little bit.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:58.010
So meaningful conflict just means conflict
that is related to the overall goal of the

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.010
story.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:04.339
Every story begins with something that makes
us go “Oh…here’s what needs to be solved.

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:08.039
Here is the outcome that is being pursued
that I’m hopefully emotionally invested.

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:10.420
I want to see them reach that outcome.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:16.960
So meaning conflict would be conflict in scenes
then which have to do with that outcome and

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.329
how’s it going trying to get to that outcome
because you could have conflict that doesn’t

00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:24.753
really relate to the larger story goal, it
would just be conflict that wouldn’t be

00:01:24.753 --> 00:01:25.753
so meaningful.

00:01:25.753 --> 00:01:28.509
It would just be conflict for the sake of
conflict.

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:32.939
Ideally it’s conflict that matters to the
story and you might also have a “B” story

00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:36.979
like there’s a conflicted relationship with
somebody who is the ally or the love interest

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:40.200
and so you might have meaningful conflict
that is advancing the “B” story.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.419
It doesn’t always have to be about what
the “A” story or goal is.

00:01:44.419 --> 00:01:48.530
But to me the most important thing is you
do need constant conflict which means constant

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:53.700
problems and it should be meaningful to whatever
we’re all there to try and see and work

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.700
out.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.009
Film Courage: What about when people put too
much conflict right away.

00:01:59.009 --> 00:02:02.849
I know using this film is really dating myself
but the movie STAYING ALIVE.

00:02:02.849 --> 00:02:08.869
It was great to see…it’s just such a great
film I just have to go there…when John Travolta’s

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:13.200
character is sort of strutting down the street
and he bumps into Sylvester Stallone’s [cameo

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.329
appearance] character and it shows okay this
is New York city and it’s tight and you’re

00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:23.870
up against all of these people and a little
bit contentious but it works because it shows

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:24.870
this.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:29.400
But then I’ve seen shows where there is
an argument which doesn’t belong.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.760
How do you balance this?

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.359
Erik: My feeling about the beginning of a
movie is that you’re mostly trying to get

00:02:36.359 --> 00:02:42.480
the audience to understand the main character
in their status quo life and emotionally get

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.290
invested in who they are.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.930
What there basic life situation is before
the big problem has emerged which will then

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.829
occupy the rest of the story.

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:56.340
That big problem tends to emerge as the catalyst
or the inciting event or the inciting incident,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.709
maybe about 12 pages in if you use the Save
The Cat [Author Blake Snyder] page count.

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:05.450
Once that thing happens that rocks their world
and sets them on a mission ultimately for

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:09.090
the rest of the story to revolve whatever
happened in that catalyst.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.780
Prior to the catalyst you are just introducing
the audience to the main character and their

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.150
world.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.780
And you’re hopefully getting them understanding
enough about that character that they can

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.819
start to feel that they know them and they
know their situation.

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:25.340
Writers often have issues with this where
they don’t really do that very well.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.560
They don’t stay with the main character
and take the time to illustrate what their

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.760
current life is and make them emotionally
accessible to the audience, make us have a

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.450
reason to want to follow them.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:40.471
That term ‘save the cat,’ the idea that
if they save a cat in the first 10 pages we

00:03:40.471 --> 00:03:46.430
will care ago them is kind of half joking
but the idea that we get invested in the character

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.980
for some reason that the audience is led to
by the writer.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:55.719
Give us a reason to care because we tend to
not care about some stranger in a movie unless

00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:57.120
you make us care.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.569
So that’s the writers most first and foremost
job, make us care and so it starts at the

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:04.379
beginning.

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:08.680
To me the opening scenes of a movie the first
ten pages or so should have conflict in them

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:10.950
because every scene should have conflict in
them.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:17.419
But that scene should have the normal conflict
of that character’s everyday life kind of

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:20.329
up until the catalyst hits.

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:24.210
Even a movie like SAVING PRIVATE RYAN which
opens with the biggest conflict you can imagine

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:30.169
(the storming of Omaha Beach) ultimately that
is just illustrating what Tom Hanks’ life

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:33.060
was like right before the catalyst of the
movie.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:37.990
The catalyst being he gets the order that
you’ve got to go save Private Ryan, right?

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.000
That sets I motion the real story problem
for the rest of the movie (the outcome that

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.650
we all become invested in).

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:47.020
Prior to that we’re all seeing well what’s
Captain Miller’s life and his unit’s life

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.710
like before they get that order?

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.800
How do we get up to speed with who he is and
what he’s dealing with and what’s going

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.110
on in his life right now?

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:57.530
He’s got a very special version of that
but that’s kind of the same thing for every

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:58.610
movie.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:02.729
So you can have conflict, you should have
conflict but sometimes people want to open

00:05:02.729 --> 00:05:06.870
with something really big where they are already
launching the story on page one.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:10.449
In other words like the catalyst even is already
happening at the very beginning before we

00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:14.950
have any idea who is who or what the world
of the story is and I think that tends to

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:19.800
be a mistake because the audience needs some
time to get up to speed and get invested first.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.710
Film Courage: Right and even with let’s
say BIG LITTLE LIES you have Reese Witherspoon’s

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:33.139
character and it’s a very manicured life
but there are these little dilemmas throughout

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:38.000
like going through the school carpool and
someone is in her way and she’s not getting

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.449
what she wants from her child and you see
these little mini-conflicts being set up even

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:46.470
though it doesn’t seem like the same intensity
as a war scene.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:52.190
Erik: Yes, conflict but not the one big conflict
that’s going to drive the rest of the story

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:53.190
from that point forward.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:58.380
And in a story like that you’re also doing
that times four different characters I think.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.639
Where each one of those characters gets some
of those scenes in the beginning which makes

00:06:01.639 --> 00:06:03.770
us go here’s this person.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.199
Here is their life situation, here’s the
basic conflicts for them.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:12.759
Here’s their basic desires and what’s
the way of those desires.

00:06:12.759 --> 00:06:16.590
Nothing huge has done anything to rock their
world and start the story but I know who they

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:17.590
are.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:21.099
I get who Nicole Kidman is, I get who Shailene
Woodley is, etc.

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:27.070
That’s part of why writing a pilot can be
harder because you have to set up multiple

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:28.460
characters and you gave to do it quickly.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:33.039
So you have to find the right way to make
the audience go “I get who they are and

00:06:33.039 --> 00:06:35.750
I’m kind of intrigued and I kind of care
about them a little.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.229
I want to see where life is going for them.”

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:43.591
Whereas in a movie you usually only have one
main character and just 10-12 pages to do

00:06:43.591 --> 00:06:44.639
it just for that.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:45.780
You don’t have to do it for anyone else.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.129
In fact you shouldn’t be trying to spend
times on scenes for other characters in the

00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:55.030
first 10 pages in my view because the whole
goal is to get us caring about the main character

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:59.629
because we’re going to subjectively their
point of view.

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:02.360
So it’s not about showing us those other
people and what is going on with them, it’s

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:07.220
about get us inside of their world and their
psychology looking out through their eyes

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:08.680
as they live their life.

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:13.290
Film Courage: Right so going back to STAYING
ALIVE, so John Travolta checks in “Any messages?”

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:20.319
He’s staying in this weekly motel where
he’s a struggling actor/dancer.

00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:23.200
And of course the guy [at the front desk]
is “No messages.”

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.560
So it just shows here is this guy [our main
character] humble and broke and that sets

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.680
off his world because that’s basically before
he meets the Finola Hughes character [the

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:33.680
main star of a Broadway show].

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.810
ErikL Yes, it’s this is my normal life and
here are the problems in my normal life that

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:42.830
are just the status quo situation and it makes
you care about him because he’s got this

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.600
undeserved misfortune (you always feel for
the underdog).

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.909
The person who wants something that they can’t
have where they are working toward and nobody

00:07:48.909 --> 00:07:49.909
cares.

00:07:49.909 --> 00:07:54.590
So that’s classic way to make an audience
want to invest in those opening pages.

